1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,260
Imagine you're standing in a laboratory.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:05,480
In front of you is a
small metallic sphere.

3
00:00:06,199 --> 00:00:11,519
It doesn't look particularly dangerous.
There is no fire. No explosion.

4
00:00:11,599 --> 00:00:16,819
No warning siren. Then, suddenly— a
flash of blue light fills the room.

5
00:00:17,373 --> 00:00:21,413
For a fraction of a second, something
has happened that you cannot see.

6
00:00:22,035 --> 00:00:26,135
The scientist standing closest to it
immediately knows what went wrong.

7
00:00:26,735 --> 00:00:28,515
He throws the equipment away.

8
00:00:28,815 --> 00:00:31,215
And everyone in the
room runs for the door.

9
00:00:31,595 --> 00:00:33,675
Nine days later, he is dead.

10
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:35,995
His name was Louis Slotin.

11
00:00:36,153 --> 00:00:40,373
And the object in front of him would
later become known as the Demon Core.

12
00:00:40,606 --> 00:00:42,866
But what actually happened
in that laboratory?

13
00:00:43,686 --> 00:00:46,246
And where did that strange
blue light come from?

14
00:00:48,646 --> 00:00:52,566
Slotin knew this work was
dangerous. He had been warned.

15
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:55,767
Enrico Fermi reportedly
told him that if he kept

16
00:00:55,997 --> 00:00:59,087
doing it this way, he would
be dead within a year.

17
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:01,739
Richard Feynman once compared this kind of

18
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:04,439
work to tickling the tail
of a sleeping dragon.

19
00:01:04,728 --> 00:01:08,308
And this wasn't experimental
plutonium made for a laboratory.

20
00:01:09,168 --> 00:01:10,608
This was a bomb core.

21
00:01:10,847 --> 00:01:13,327
It had been made for
the third atomic bomb.

22
00:01:14,058 --> 00:01:16,498
On the day of the accident,
Slotin was teaching

23
00:01:16,728 --> 00:01:19,358
the man who was about
to take over his job.

24
00:01:19,978 --> 00:01:22,318
The experiment wasn't even scheduled.

25
00:01:22,998 --> 00:01:25,418
Slotin decided to do
it as a demonstration.

26
00:01:26,476 --> 00:01:31,096
The approved method used spacers, so the
assembly could never close completely.

27
00:01:31,636 --> 00:01:33,376
Slotin took them out.

28
00:01:33,977 --> 00:01:36,817
His way of doing it
depended on a screwdriver.

29
00:01:37,214 --> 00:01:41,494
So why was a screwdriver anywhere
near a nearly critical plutonium core?

30
00:01:42,310 --> 00:01:45,790
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

31
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:48,030
It really helps the channel.

32
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,340
One fission releases neutrons.

33
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:55,260
Those neutrons can trigger more fissions.

34
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:59,985
If enough of them keep the process going,
the reaction becomes self-sustaining.

35
00:02:00,355 --> 00:02:02,555
The system is called critical.

36
00:02:02,951 --> 00:02:06,771
If too few neutrons remain
available, the reaction dies out.

37
00:02:07,391 --> 00:02:11,371
But if the number grows— the reaction
can increase extremely rapidly.

38
00:02:11,715 --> 00:02:15,695
The scientists at Los Alamos
were studying precisely how close

39
00:02:15,925 --> 00:02:19,495
they could bring a piece of
plutonium to that dangerous boundary.

40
00:02:20,131 --> 00:02:23,091
Was the core dangerous
even without a screwdriver?

41
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:25,651
Far less than you might think.

42
00:02:26,391 --> 00:02:31,031
On its own, it was below critical mass,
so most of its neutrons simply escaped.

43
00:02:31,803 --> 00:02:36,963
Its radiation was mostly alpha particles,
and a thin nickel coating stopped them.

44
00:02:37,623 --> 00:02:39,803
The sphere just felt warm to the touch.

45
00:02:40,498 --> 00:02:44,918
The danger began only when something
sent those escaping neutrons back.

46
00:02:45,122 --> 00:02:47,742
Around it, scientists
could place materials that

47
00:02:47,972 --> 00:02:50,462
reflected neutrons back
toward the plutonium.

48
00:02:51,382 --> 00:02:53,742
One such material was beryllium.

49
00:02:54,223 --> 00:02:57,843
Move the reflector closer— and
the system becomes more reactive.

50
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:01,803
Move it away— and the system
becomes less reactive.

51
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:04,434
And with beryllium wrapped
almost all the way around

52
00:03:04,664 --> 00:03:07,954
the core, even a tiny gap
made all the difference.

53
00:03:08,337 --> 00:03:11,857
And there was no reliable way to
do this by remote control yet.

54
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:14,137
So it was done by hand.

55
00:03:16,677 --> 00:03:20,517
On May 21, 1946, Louis Slotin was

56
00:03:20,747 --> 00:03:23,097
performing exactly this
kind of experiment.

57
00:03:23,834 --> 00:03:27,414
Slotin was a Canadian physicist
working at Los Alamos.

58
00:03:27,814 --> 00:03:30,614
He had performed similar
experiments before.

59
00:03:31,292 --> 00:03:33,512
The core sat inside a
beryllium half-shell.

60
00:03:33,742 --> 00:03:38,232
Slotin slowly lowered a second shell
over it, holding it with his thumb.

61
00:03:38,855 --> 00:03:41,635
The shells could almost
completely surround it.

62
00:03:41,915 --> 00:03:44,815
But he needed to keep a
tiny gap between them.

63
00:03:44,953 --> 00:03:46,693
So he used a screwdriver.

64
00:03:47,145 --> 00:03:50,685
The screwdriver wasn't some
sophisticated safety mechanism.

65
00:03:51,485 --> 00:03:54,285
It was literally a
screwdriver in Slotin's hand.

66
00:03:55,144 --> 00:03:57,004
Then— the screwdriver slipped.

67
00:03:57,379 --> 00:03:59,479
The upper shell dropped into place.

68
00:04:00,248 --> 00:04:03,328
For an instant, the plutonium
became supercritical.

69
00:04:03,408 --> 00:04:06,168
And something extraordinary happened.

70
00:04:06,474 --> 00:04:09,354
A burst of radiation passed
through the laboratory.

71
00:04:10,134 --> 00:04:12,494
Witnesses reported seeing a blue flash.

72
00:04:13,731 --> 00:04:17,771
Slotin immediately flipped the upper
shell off. The reaction stopped.

73
00:04:18,467 --> 00:04:22,047
The entire event had lasted
only a fraction of a second.

74
00:04:22,127 --> 00:04:24,807
But the damage had already been done.

75
00:04:27,507 --> 00:04:29,487
There was no nuclear explosion.

76
00:04:30,187 --> 00:04:33,407
The plutonium core did not
explode like an atomic bomb.

77
00:04:33,691 --> 00:04:37,371
Instead, it produced an extremely
intense burst of radiation.

78
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:40,885
For a fraction of a second,
the chain reaction ran

79
00:04:41,115 --> 00:04:44,605
away on its own, faster
than any human could react.

80
00:04:44,981 --> 00:04:48,961
But for the people standing nearby,
a fraction of a second was enough.

81
00:04:49,243 --> 00:04:52,403
Slotin had been standing
closest to the core.

82
00:04:52,563 --> 00:04:54,983
He received a massive dose of radiation.

83
00:04:55,890 --> 00:04:58,190
The other scientists in
the room also received

84
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:02,330
radiation, but their exposure
was considerably smaller.

85
00:05:03,047 --> 00:05:06,187
Slotin knew immediately that
he had been badly exposed.

86
00:05:07,047 --> 00:05:11,367
One of the men in the room later said:
"He knew he had had it at that time."

87
00:05:11,965 --> 00:05:13,945
Even so, he sketched
where everyone had been

88
00:05:14,175 --> 00:05:17,305
standing, so the doctors
could work out their doses.

89
00:05:18,119 --> 00:05:20,399
He had survived the immediate accident.

90
00:05:20,499 --> 00:05:22,999
But he would not survive its consequences.

91
00:05:25,889 --> 00:05:28,449
The flash looked like Cherenkov radiation.

92
00:05:28,956 --> 00:05:33,787
That is the blue glow you can see
around nuclear reactor fuel under water.

93
00:05:34,087 --> 00:05:36,807
It happens when charged
particles move through a material

94
00:05:37,037 --> 00:05:40,447
faster than light can travel
through that material.

95
00:05:40,716 --> 00:05:43,916
But there was a problem.
This happened in air.

96
00:05:44,233 --> 00:05:47,873
In air, light travels almost
as fast as it does in a vacuum.

97
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:50,813
Hardly anything can outrun it there.

98
00:05:51,349 --> 00:05:54,549
The burst of radiation
ionised the surrounding air.

99
00:05:54,629 --> 00:05:58,609
And excited air molecules
give off light of their own.

100
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:01,290
So the blue flash wasn't the
thing that killed Slotin.

101
00:06:02,029 --> 00:06:05,389
It was the warning that the
invisible thing had already happened.

102
00:06:08,439 --> 00:06:10,939
He was conscious when
he reached the hospital.

103
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:15,548
But the radiation had already fatally
damaged cells throughout his body.

104
00:06:16,145 --> 00:06:19,165
Over the following days,
his condition deteriorated.

105
00:06:20,273 --> 00:06:23,333
Nine days after the
accident, Louis Slotin died.

106
00:06:23,873 --> 00:06:26,053
He was only thirty-five years old.

107
00:06:28,813 --> 00:06:31,073
And this wasn't even the first time.

108
00:06:31,325 --> 00:06:35,025
The same plutonium core had
already caused a fatal accident.

109
00:06:35,795 --> 00:06:39,495
Less than a year earlier, another
scientist named Harry Daghlian

110
00:06:39,575 --> 00:06:43,215
had been performing a criticality
experiment with the same core.

111
00:06:43,805 --> 00:06:48,025
He was using tungsten-carbide bricks
to reflect neutrons toward it.

112
00:06:48,509 --> 00:06:51,869
One of the bricks slipped from his
hand and fell onto the assembly.

113
00:06:52,589 --> 00:06:53,789
The core went supercritical.

114
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:59,570
A guard sat a few metres away, reading
a newspaper with his back to the table.

115
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:02,890
The blue flash lit up
the page in his hands.

116
00:07:04,139 --> 00:07:06,019
Daghlian immediately removed the brick.

117
00:07:06,343 --> 00:07:08,983
But he had received a
lethal dose of radiation.

118
00:07:09,783 --> 00:07:11,843
He died twenty-five days later.

119
00:07:14,203 --> 00:07:18,063
Both fatal accidents happened on
the twenty-first day of a month.

120
00:07:18,693 --> 00:07:20,753
One in August 1945.

121
00:07:21,253 --> 00:07:23,773
The other in May 1946.

122
00:07:24,786 --> 00:07:27,366
The coincidence meant
nothing scientifically.

123
00:07:27,786 --> 00:07:31,126
But it made the story
almost impossible to forget.

124
00:07:32,239 --> 00:07:35,199
It wasn't called the Demon
Core when Slotin died.

125
00:07:35,855 --> 00:07:38,515
The name came later,
after the same piece of

126
00:07:38,745 --> 00:07:41,675
plutonium had been involved
in two fatal accidents.

127
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:44,320
One core. Two scientists.

128
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:46,080
Two criticality accidents.

129
00:07:46,527 --> 00:07:49,047
And both times, the warning came too late.

130
00:07:51,587 --> 00:07:55,927
The accidents changed the way scientists
approached criticality experiments.

131
00:07:57,113 --> 00:07:59,233
Hands-on experiments
like this were banned.

132
00:07:59,992 --> 00:08:01,597
Eventually, they were run from about four

133
00:08:01,827 --> 00:08:04,732
hundred metres away,
watched on television.

134
00:08:05,528 --> 00:08:07,968
Scientists became much more cautious about

135
00:08:08,198 --> 00:08:11,248
bringing fissile material
close to criticality.

136
00:08:11,747 --> 00:08:14,547
The core had been made
for the third atomic bomb.

137
00:08:14,813 --> 00:08:19,293
After the second accident, the core was
too radioactive to use straight away.

138
00:08:20,008 --> 00:08:24,208
It was moved to a third test at
Bikini — and that test was cancelled.

139
00:08:24,968 --> 00:08:28,008
The age of scientists manually
holding pieces of equipment

140
00:08:28,238 --> 00:08:32,708
around a nearly critical plutonium
core was coming to an end.

141
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:38,180
The Demon Core itself was eventually
melted down and its plutonium reused.

142
00:08:38,627 --> 00:08:43,327
So the infamous sphere no longer
exists. But the story survived.

143
00:08:45,637 --> 00:08:46,897
Remember the blue flash?

144
00:08:48,058 --> 00:08:52,158
It wasn't an explosion. It
wasn't the plutonium burning.

145
00:08:52,913 --> 00:08:55,813
It wasn't even the thing
that killed Louis Slotin.

146
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:59,760
It was the moment the invisible
danger became visible.

147
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:04,010
And all it took was a screwdriver
slipping a few millimetres.
